Kernir patch 0006: the gzip filter checks each member's trailer, and tar reads to the end of the stream

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_filter_gzip.c (struct gzip,
consume_trailer, gzip_filter_read) and libarchive/archive_read_support_format_tar.c (tar_read_header,
the end-of-archive branch).

Upstream inflates each gzip member and consumes its 8-byte trailer unread ("XXX TODO: Verify the
length and CRC"), so damaged data extracted without an error and a repack wrote it into a new archive
with a fresh checksum. gzip_filter_read now keeps a CRC32 of each member's output (gzip->crc, reset per
member in consume_header) and consume_trailer compares it and the member's length mod 2^32 (total_out,
taken before inflateEnd) with the trailer. A mismatch is ARCHIVE_FATAL "gzip trailer CRC mismatch" or
"gzip ISIZE mismatch" (under #ifndef DONT_FAIL_ON_CRC_ERROR, like the other readers' CRC checks). The
read that met the trailer still hands out what it decompressed and every later read fails, so a
stream that ends inside one 64 KiB block keeps its data flow and fails on the next read (upstream's
"Truncated gzip input" returns at once instead). Each member of a multi-member stream is checked.

The tar part: a compressed tar's trailer (gzip CRC32/ISIZE, the bzip2 stream CRC, the xz stream footer)
sits after tar's end-of-archive blocks. Upstream tar stops at the first two null blocks, so with more
than one filter read of output left (a long record padding, or simply a stream past the bidders' read-
ahead) the trailer was never read, and a filter error while reading the second null block was cleared
to EOF. Now, after the null blocks, when tar reads through a filter (a->filter->upstream != NULL: any
compression filter, uudecode, or a chain of them; the client reader alone has no upstream), it reads
and discards the rest of the filter's output, so the top filter reaches its end and checks its trailer;
a filter error there is ARCHIVE_FATAL with the filter's message. What a filter itself accepts after its
stream (concatenated streams, xz stream padding, zstd skippable frames, zero padding after a gzip,
bzip2, xz or lzma stream) still reads as before. A plain tar is not drained: it stops at its end blocks
as upstream does, so a long tail (a zero-filled disk image the tar bidder takes, a tar followed by a
large payload, an endless non-seekable input) is not read. Bytes after the end-of-archive blocks are
never parsed (unlike the read_concatenated_archives option, which would refuse a tar with junk there).

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0006-gzip-trailer-check-tar-reads-to-end.patch

--- a/libarchive/archive_read_support_filter_gzip.c
+++ b/libarchive/archive_read_support_filter_gzip.c
